  {
    "episode_id": 97313,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/7/97313.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 33,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, it's been wild. It's been kind of like I thought it would be. It's pretty disappointing. I kind of knew that it would be that way. There's a lot of corruption. It's every bit of swampy as you would think it is. It's just, it's, it's just disappointing in many ways just because you know that the country screwed up, you know that politicians in DC don't care what you think. And then you, you know, you get to go there and you get to watch it in real time. And it's, it's hard to see man because you know the implications of it. You know that The implications are that the things that need to get fixed for the people of this country aren't going to get fixed because the machine up in Washington, DC, that absorbs the men and women that go there with good intentions on the regular, it doesn't care what the people want. All it cares is about protecting the machine and keeping that power in place, man. You can call it the swamp, the uni-party, the cartel, whatever you want to call it. There's a bunch of names for it, but the bottom line is, is it's really a system. And unfortunately, it's often Democrats and Republicans that work together, you know, and it usually doesn't benefit the people. So it's unfortunate. It's a pain in the ass, but, you know, I'm there and I'm grateful to be there. And there are some pretty solid people up there. It is a minority of folks, but, you know, I feel blessed, but frustrated at the same time if that makes any sense.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "The political system in Washington prioritizes self-preservation over public welfare.",
        "confidence": 0.97
      },
