    "turn_idx": 63,
    "turn_text": "goes in and doesn't drink the kool-aid and so you know it's it's it is a mess guys but you know i'm trying to just paint in broad colors so that your listeners and your viewers kind of you know understand and clark back to what you were talking about with the specific bills one of the things that we fought for back in january of last year When I first got into Congress, we had a big speaker fight, which hadn't taken place in like 150 years. Normally, they just say, hey, this is going to be the leader and everybody vote for him. 20 of us out of, there's 435-ish members of Congress. There are about 225 Republicans. And 20 of us said, no, we're not doing that. This guy is not fit to lead. Our voters don't want him. He's a swamp creature. We're not, we're not doing it. And so that went on for, you know, I can't remember how many rounds it was. It was like 15 rounds and it started at 20 and it started whittling down. And I was one of the last six people that. never voted for the guy, some of the people that made a deal so that Kevin McCarthy could become speaker. Clark, one of the elements of the deal was a 72-hour rule, and a reinstatement of that rule so that once a bill got dropped, We all had 72 hours to read it, right? Because to your point, it's one of the techniques that the swamp uses to drop this massive piece of legislation, and then your representatives don't even have time to read it, and they've got to vote on it, right? And so that's one of, it's one of their mini tools that they use. One of the things that, we fought for in that package or my group fought for that in that package was something called single subject your main bills in that you know the bill whatever it was it couldn't have it couldn't have you know, be this Christmas tree, as we call it, with a bunch of different branches and a bunch of different verticals. It had to be single subject and everything in the bill had to be germane to what the bill was about. Because a lot of the way that we fund and run our government right now is either through CR, which stands for continuing resolution, just, hey, we're going to fund the government at the exact same level that we've been funding it at with the exact same programs. And sadly, that's even a win from what usually happens. Usually what happens is something called an omnibus or a minibus. And that's like all 12 of our appropriations bills or, you know, six or seven of them in a minibus all at one time. And, you know, the way we funded the government most recently was through minibus's. So you would have like a thousand and four page bill 15 pages of it would be good. And then the rest of it would be crap at, you know, at increases of last year's level. So you're still spending more money than we have, way more money than we have. It easily $2 trillion a year more than we have. You're continuing to fund the woken weaponized programs within the federal government, right? And it's just like, And the vast majority of people on both sides of the aisle vote for this crap. And so it's frustrating, man, but that was one of those are a couple of the things that we fought for in the January speaker fight. And then once some of us realized that Kevin McCarthy wouldn't hold his keep to his word and honor the deal that he made, eight of us, only eight of us. in the entire Congress moved to fire the Speaker of the House or vacate the chair. That's what they call it. And so you can imagine, and that's never happened before in the entire history of our country. And so you can imagine how riled up that hornet's nest was,",
