    "turn_idx": 84,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, you know, there, there is, there is truth to that. And you're right, you know, money backs a lot of it and it's behind a lot of it. But I do want to encourage maybe people that are listening to your show up in commerce. One of the things that I found is that if you actually go to Washington DC and you go with principles and you say, hey, I'm gonna fight these principles and people actually see you do that, what I found Clark is that people will reward you with support. Like I just won my primary 81 to 29. That's really tough to do in politics, especially when the powers that be Um, we're, we're setting aside three to $5 million for any, anybody that could take me out or they, you know, thought could build the momentum. But so it's just like, um, the, it is true that a lot of money is behind the machine and it's act of lights, if you will. But one thing that a lot of people I don't think realizes is that when you actually go, if you're able to get into the machine and, and you go and you're willing to fight for the people and actually stand up. to the swamp, the people of this country, they will reward you for that. And it doesn't mean it's going to be easy, but I think a lot of politicians just fall in line because they think that it's, it's futile if you don't fall in line. To an extent it is, honestly, because like, even for a guy like me, I'll never be the chairman of a committee. And a lot of the time, just like in military or anywhere else or in business, the company goes the direction the CEO wants it to go. The military goes the direction the commanding officer wants it to go. Your congressional committees, they go the direction that the committee chairman wanted to go. And so I think a lot of politicians get into it with good intentions and they say, well, I'm going to fight for the people and I'm going to do what they want. But first, I've got to climb the ladder a little bit. I've got to get a little seniority before I can actually make a real difference. And so I think there are good intention people to go to Washington with that mentality. And then unfortunately, six, eight years go by, they finally get that committee chairman and they get that gavel and they've They forgot knew they are and they don't want to leave the ranking, the leadership spot.",
