    "turn_text": "fixed. Yeah, I think the biggest thing that would have to happen is the American people would have to wake up. You guys would be so surprised if you found out what percentage of people actually vote in elections at primaries. The turnout in a primary, who you're going to vote for on your side of the aisle, is super low. You'll have probably, roughly, I don't know, I'm just throwing out numbers like spitballing here, but let's just say, Let's just say, in your district, you represent 750, 800,000 people. So let's say roughly half of those are on your side, whether you're a Democrat or Republican, right? So you're probably talking about 400,000 to 350,000 people. It's super normal in a primary for only 70,000 of those 350,000 to turn out in boat. Right? So it's like, we can all sit around the water cooling complaint, but if you're not going to even show up to vote. shut your mouth, right? I don't want to hear it. If you can't even take the time to go vote, what are we talking about? If you can't take the time to go show up to the Republican Party in your area or the Democrat Party in your area and listen to the speakers or the candidates and go to a candidate forum or a debate or something along those lines, then what are we talking about here? So again, it's going to take the people to wake up and One of my favorite opponents is a guy named Dan Vongino. I think he's one of the best to listen to right now because he's wicked smart. He was a Secret Service agent. So like all this stuff that happened on The 13th with the assassination attempt, I think Dan Bongino is one of the best voices to listen to. But he talks about, he ran for office three times and he never won. And I think it's almost a blessing that he didn't win because his voice now is so powerful around the country. But he talks about watching being a cop in New York before Rudy Giuliani came in back in like, I think it was like the 90s. And he talked about how bad New York was and he's like, it was just out of control. And he saw it gets so bad. People felt enough pain that they were finally willing to look outside of their own bias, their own, you know, echo chambers and be like, Hey, we got to try some new because this isn't working. And he says on the show all the time, I guess we haven't felt enough pain yet. And so I think that Americans are starting to feel that pain with record inflation, somebody in their life or a loved one close to them has either died of fentanyl or maybe had a negative experience with somebody in this country who wasn't supposed to be here. They're seeing the headlines. When they pump gas or they're buying groceries, they're struggling to make ends meet. They're wondering why the hell are we sending you know, hundreds of billions of dollars over to a country that most people can't find on a map and escalate a war with the biggest nuclear superpower in the world. Maybe we should be talking about peace talks. And I'm seeing this right now in the presidential election, you know, especially with like minority men who are coming over to Donald Trump and they're like,",
